---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/migration";
const title = "Shopify Email Migration \u2014 State Mapping and Cutover";
const desc = "Move email platforms with reconciled identities, suppressions, flow ownership and a reversible cutover.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Should both platforms send the same flow during testing?",
    "answer": "Use shadow evaluation or exclusive routing. Duplicate live delivery is not a valid comparison method."
  },
  {
    "question": "How are old suppressions handled?",
    "answer": "Carry their meaning and evidence into enforceable destination rules, then test those rules. Importing a list must not reset opt-outs."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should the old account be cancelled?",
    "answer": "After exports, rollback needs, retention requirements and contract terms are reconciled and the responsible owner signs off."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does migration prove a cheaper platform is better?",
    "answer": "No. Compare the validated workflow, full transition costs and common measurement definitions before accepting a business case."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Define what is moving</h2>
    <p>This guide is for an operator replacing an existing sender. Inventory contacts, channel preferences, consent evidence, custom fields, events, templates, discounts, queued messages and reporting exports. Mark each item as migrating, rebuilding, archiving or staying with its current owner. Export historical metric definitions as well as totals. A new vendor’s attributed revenue is not directly comparable when tracking windows or eligible populations differ.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Map identity and state before importing</h2>
    <p>Create a mapping table with source identifier, destination identifier, field type, meaning, transformation, missing-value policy and validation case. Email alone may not resolve merged profiles, changed addresses or guest orders. Reconcile sample records and aggregate counts, recording deliberate exclusions. Preserve opt-out and bounce restrictions in a form the new sender can enforce. Do not treat an import as renewed permission or delete suppression evidence to make a list smaller.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Translate rules, not just labels</h2>
    <p>For each live segment write its predicate in plain language, then reproduce it against destination data. Check date boundaries, time zones, currency, order status and null values. A source tag meaning repeat buyer is not equivalent to a destination lifetime-spend rule. Test profiles immediately inside and outside each boundary. Record any rule the new builder cannot represent and decide whether to redesign, retain that workflow elsewhere or reject the migration.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Partition delivery while comparing behavior</h2>
    <p>Parallel validation should not mean sending duplicate messages. In a proposed shadow phase, evaluate destination decisions without customer dispatch and compare them with the incumbent. For a live pilot, assign mutually exclusive cohorts or entire message purposes to one sender. Document routing, queued-message handling and the exact cutover time. Test an unsubscribe in each still-active system and confirm all marketing paths stop according to the agreed policy. Stop expansion if state propagation is unreliable.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Treat the sender transition as its own workstream</h2>
    <p>Confirm domain authentication, reply handling and tracking links with the chosen providers. Agree a volume transition with the sending provider based on the actual history and intended audience; a fixed fourteen-day schedule is not an acceptance standard. Monitor delivery failures, complaints and available reputation signals alongside dispatch correctness. Avoid stacking the sender change with a major list expansion or unrelated channel migration, which makes diagnosis harder.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Make rollback state-aware</h2>
    <p>Before cutover, retain permitted access to the incumbent and export the material needed to restore operation. Record the last event processed and messages already sent by each system. If rollback is needed, pause the destination first, reconcile late purchases and opt-outs, cancel conflicting queues, then restore only the intended incumbent paths. Do not replay an entire historical event stream. A functional rollback needs current suppression state, not just old templates.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Acceptance and account retirement</h2>
    <p>Sign off mapped counts with explained differences, edge-case records, exclusive sender ownership, unsubscribe propagation and representative live events. Compare business metrics using a common order ledger and clearly defined windows; attribution differences alone do not prove improvement or damage. Retire the old account only when the responsible owner confirms export completeness, contractual notice, required record retention and removal of unused access. Timing is a project decision, not a universal thirty-day rule.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Evidence boundaries</h2>
    <p>Shopify’s Order reference notes that older order access requires additional scope beyond the default history window. Check historical coverage before promising to reconstruct repeat-purchase segments. The Customer reference describes customer purchase history and preferences; neither reference certifies a vendor’s import behavior. Sequenzy and other candidates should demonstrate the mapping and rollback requirements using the intended connector, with unsupported paths logged as migration blockers.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><p>Source scope: <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Order">Shopify Order reference</a> and <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Customer">Customer reference</a>, checked November 24, 2026. Supplier capabilities and legal conclusions are outside this source scope.</p></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><p>Related: <a class="link" href="/guides/implementation-plan">Shopify Email Implementation Plan</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">Shopify Email Pricing Models</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/replenishment-tools">Shopify Replenishment Tools</a></p></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
